  "question": {
    "text": "Two cells, having the same E.M.F. are connected in series through an external resistance <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math></em>. Cells have internal resistances <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math> respectively. When the circuit is closed, the potential difference across the first cell is zero. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> is",
    "image": null
  },
